# Code quality

## Readability

- Prefer explicit names over abbreviations (`packet_number`, not `pn`).
- Keep functions short; extract when a block needs its own docstring.
- Comments explain *why* (device quirks, LED, ACK timeouts), never narrate *what*.
- No dead code, leftover TODOs without an issue, or commented-out experiments in PRs.

## Python (integration core)

- Type-annotate public functions and dataclasses.
- Use `async` end-to-end for BLE; never block the event loop with image work of large size without `asyncio.to_thread` (or equivalent).
- Raise typed/domain errors (`TransferError`, `ProtocolError`); map to HA user messages at the boundary.
- Match Home Assistant style for integration entrypoints (`async_setup_entry`, config flow).

## TypeScript (Lovelace / panel)

- Small focused modules; share types with clear contracts to backend services.
- English-only user-facing strings in MVP (HA `translations/` when wiring i18n later).

## Tests & review

- Protocol encode/decode and image packing **must** have unit tests (port fixtures from the web app where possible).
- BLE transfer logic: unit-test ACK/retry with a fake characteristic; hardware tests optional/manual.
- PRs should be reviewable in isolation: one concern per PR when practical.
